<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Nash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Walter Nash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Nash was a British-born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> politician who was Prime Minister from 1957 to 1960, leading the Second Labour Government. As finance minister from 1935 to 1949 he had helped build the welfare state of the First Labour Government. His own government's 1958 Black Budget, which raised taxes on beer, cigarettes and cars to meet a balance-of-payments crisis, contributed to its defeat in 1960.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-nash.jpg" alt="Walter Nash" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walter_Nash_(ca_1940s).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nash was born on 12 February 1882 in Kidderminster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a>, into a poor family with an alcoholic father, and won a scholarship to King Charles I Grammar School that the family could not afford to take up. He worked as a clerk and kept a shop after marrying Lottie May Eaton in 1906, but illness, the death of a daughter at birth and a recession led the family to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, where he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> in 1909.</p><p>There his Christian faith merged with socialism, and through friends such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-joseph-savage">Michael Joseph Savage</a>, Bob Semple and Harry Holland he became a Christian socialist and a pacifist. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> his open <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacifism">pacifism</a> led police to scrutinise his mail for evidence of sedition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Nash helped found the New Plymouth branch of the Labour Party in 1918, was fined in 1921 for importing seditious literature after a tour of European socialist conferences, and became the party's national secretary in 1922. He won the Hutt seat at a by-election in 1929 and held it for 13 consecutive terms, over 38 years. As minister of finance he reintroduced a graduated land tax, oversaw the nationalisation of the Reserve Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and negotiated trade terms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, holding the post for 14 years, through the whole of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>Elected leader after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-fraser">Peter Fraser</a>'s death, he was ridiculed for saying of the 1951 waterfront dispute, &quot;We are not for the waterside workers, and we are not against them.&quot; As prime minister he also took foreign affairs and Māori affairs. The Black Budget, presented by Arnold Nordmeyer, angered the public, though economists praised its financial discipline.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nash resisted calls to retire, partly to keep Nordmeyer from succeeding him, and stepped down as leader only in February 1963, the first Labour leader not to die in office. He died on 4 June 1968, aged 86 and still a member of Parliament. His state funeral, the first since Fraser's in 1950, was the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> to be televised. He had been made a Knight Grand Cross of the Order of St Michael and St George in 1965, but twice declined a life peerage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walter Nash” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Long periods of National Party rule under Sidney Holland and Keith Holyoake alternated with Labour governments under <b>Walter Nash</b> and Norman Kirk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kidderminster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.39,-2.25&amp;year=1937" aria-label="Kidderminster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4499 356 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Walter Nash"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.39,-2.25&amp;year=1937">What was here in 1937? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
